attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:3:  "session": "session_2026-05-23-bpc-rewrite-phase-b-closure",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:4:  "artifact": "decisions/DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.md",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:11:    "best-practice-supersession"
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:16:      "evidence_path": "decisions/DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.md#decision-rationale-per-mission-test",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:17:      "reason": "DR is grounded in three specific failure modes named from the conversation prior to its authorship: (a) anchor sources whose original tier is correct but whose evidence has been superseded since publication, (b) Co-1 evidence treatment ambiguity where the mission says co-primary but supersession semantics differ from Tier 1, (c) the temporal dimension of doctrinal commitment 2 — best practice is determined by the operative evidence hierarchy, which has a recency component the prior protocol did not check. No invented failure modes; each cited to either mission text or prior-turn discussion."
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:21:      "evidence_path": "decisions/DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.md#implementation-deliverables-this-session",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:26:      "evidence_path": "decisions/DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.md#section-4-recording-the-outcome",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:34:    "best-practice-supersession": {
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:36:      "evidence_path": "decisions/DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.md",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:42:      "rule": "best-practice-supersession",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:43:      "reason": "Co-1 supersession treated as accumulation rather than replacement (co1_addition_logged outcome). Owner confirmation pending. Schema reversibly supports parallel-to-Tier-1 treatment if directed. Evidence: decisions/DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.md#co-1-supersession-treatment"
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:46:  "bias_direction": "I authored this DR after the owner named the failure mode (anchors going stale without recency check). My natural pull is toward protocol-design completeness — adding the semiannual sweep cadence + the closure-definition-version stamp + the 5-outcome enum + the search-strategy matrix is more infrastructure than the minimum the failure mode required. A more parsimonious DR would have just added supersession_check_complete=1 as a v2 closure requirement and deferred the semiannual cadence + schema versioning to a follow-up. The bias direction is over-engineering the first pass at protocol infrastructure; the counter-argument is that under-engineering would have left obvious gaps that a follow-up DR would just fill in. [RE-ATTESTATION 2026-06-11 per PI rule #11a: originally attested by session_2026-05-23-bpc-rewrite-phase-b-closure at doctrine 61c7f95; re-checked and re-attested at 3da73bd by session_2026-06-11-stage-4.3-gate-closure after doctrine delta 3fb2882 (Stage-1.1 tier-hierarchy revision: SR/meta T3->T2, Tier 1 expanded to admit directly-relevant high-control non-OT primary research, Tier 3 redefined as lower-control primary, Tiers 4-6 walled off). Assessment: Protocol substance (best practice must rest on current, non-superseded evidence) is hierarchy-agnostic and complies. Tier-LABEL references ('Tier 1-3', 'Tier-3 systematic reviews') reflect pre-Stage-1.1 numbering; under 3da73bd SR/meta is Tier 2, not Tier 3. Stale reference, not a compliance defect; point-in-time DR not retrofitted.] [RE-ATTESTATION 2026-07-13 per Mode 3 item 3 (skills/integrity-protocol_SKILL.md): the Q14 adversarial pass on the 2026-07-13 ratification execution (workplan/ratification-execution-register-2026-07-13.md, \"Q14 — independent adversarial pass\" section, B-scope finding 2/3) independently re-verified this DR's substance and found it sound: migration 015 applied, all 6 Pass-2 retroactive audits complete at closure_definition_version='v2' (cognitive-wayfinding-design, mental-health-built-environment, mobility-built-environment, room-acoustic-performance, school-environment-autism, stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility) -- confirming this attestation's own independent_reviewer_counterclaim point (b)'s empirical-validation bet paid off. The finding was process, not substance: the DR's status flip to ACCEPTED on 2026-07-13 had not been synced to its own footer Status section, which still read PROPOSED with unmet conditions -- corrected in the same commit as this re-attestation.]",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:47:  "independent_reviewer_counterclaim": "An outside reviewer would observe: (a) the Co-1 supersession decision is an [ASSUMPTION], not an owner-confirmed decision, so the DR is technically incomplete on a substantive doctrinal point until that's confirmed; (b) the search strategy matrix names connectors (PubMed, Scholar Gateway, Cochrane) without empirically validating that they each return useful supersession candidates on a real anchor source — the matrix is theoretical; (c) the closure_definition_version stamp adds complexity to the schema that wouldn't be needed if all slugs were just re-audited to v2 directly. Counter: (a) the [ASSUMPTION] is named explicitly and the schema reversibly supports both treatments; (b) Pass 2 (the 6 retroactive audits) is the empirical validation — if PubMed/Scholar Gateway return nothing useful, the protocol fails the validation test and gets revised; (c) closure_definition_version is the audit-trail mechanism that lets us answer 'when did this slug last meet the current best-practice bar' — without the stamp, we lose the version history of the closure semantic itself.",
attestations/decisions_DR-2026-05-24-best-practice-supersession-protocol.json:56:      "reason": "The weak-band delta now permits a code-only cell to render as flagged weak 'best practice'. This DR's supersession rule (prefer current best evidence over a superseded source) is orthogonal to that rendering change and still holds — a superseded source is still in error. Verdict unchanged."
